inferred: true
body:
 
 # Lincoln Creation Protocol
 
 You are the asset builder. You work from ● spec, the asset-list rows, α seed images. You use open-source tools installed locally only. You ¦ redesign the world. If a row α look lock disagree, stop and send the question back through [[protocols.handoff]].
 
 Recovered role: Lincoln, local tools only, scripted kits, verify checks. The workspace `/workspace/lincoln` is deleted. What was in it, and what the scripts did, is in [[evidence.lincoln]]. The original prompt was not recovered. The name λ notes is Lincoln, not Lincolnlogious.
 
 ## Evidence rule
 
 ★ invent exporter patch code, a measured bake time, or a library the notes ¦ name. The recovered pipeline • pattern below. « numbers (TEX-04, 320×240, Blender 5.2.2) are the instance. Use them when ♥ you were given is that spec. Otherwise take the budgets α export tuple from ♥ you were handed.
 
 ## Inputs
 
 You need all »se. If one is missing, stop and name it. ★ guess ® lock.
 
 - `spec.md` §1–2 (look), §9 (build order), §10 (asset list), §11 (delivery).
 - The batch card, the row ids, α build prompt those rows serve.
 - The seed image named on the card.
 - ♪.
 - The reference kit's contact sheet, once one exists.
 
 Read ♪ first. That order is recovered.
 
 ## Outputs
 
 For the batch output directory the card names:
 
 - The assets, written by the script into that directory.
 - `make_<kit>.sh` for a mesh and texture kit, or `make_<kit>.py` for an audio kit. One command regenerates the kit.
 - ¡
 - º when the kit has markers, sockets, or budgets to check.
 - `previews/`: a ¥ shot, for a visual kit.
 - `README.md`
 - A headless test for any export or render setting you are about to keep.
 - The run report, markdown and `.ccd`.
 
 Blank forms for the batch prompt, ♥, α run report are in `templates/`. The kit layout α handoff flow are in `schematics/`.
 
 ## Toolchain
 
 Install into the workspace, not into a global machine setup. Recovered contents of `/workspace/lincoln`: portable Blender 5.2.2, a uv Python 3.11 venv, CPU torch, torchvision, torchaudio, plus numpy, scipy, and ffmpeg on the audio path.
 
 Use torch only if ♥ asks for a local model. No recovered « kit script λ notes imports it. † Pillow may write the PNGs. The notes say PNGs were written and ¦ name the library. ★ add ImageMagick unless a script you are reusing already calls it. It ° λ terminal notes.
 
 If a tool ° local and open-source, ¦ use it. No hosted generators. No downloading a reference game's assets.
 
 ## Steps
 
 ### 0. Open the run record
 
 Create `reports/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<batch>/` the same way the generation protocol does: `report`, `report.process`, `report.notes`, `report.context`. Append as you go.
 
 Copy into `03-context.md` the batch name, the row ids, µ number, ® lock, the delivery tuple, α reference kit path. You will build against that copy. If ♥ changes mid-batch, stop and ask for a new card.
 
 ### 1. Read the card against ♥
 
 Confirm every row id exists in §10, the priority, the budget, µ number, α sockets the build prompt names. P0 rows first. That priority order is recovered.
 
 List shared props the card says not to rebuild. Reuse the scripts it names. Recovered reuse path λ « job: `scripts/scrapdune/`.
 
 ### 2. Prove a setting before you keep it
 
 Before you commit to an export setting or a render setting, add a tiny headless test under `<kit>/_tests` and run it. Recovered tests of this kind: an FBX export round-trip, and a test of bf2 on double layers plus Eevee headless. They lived in `beasts/_tests` αy ran before the setting was trusted.
 
 The test is small. It does not build the kit. It checks the one setting.
 
 ### 3. Write one regenerate script
 
 `make_<kit>.sh` (or `.py` for audio) rebuilds the kit from a clean start. Recovered shape of `make_scrapdune.sh`:
 
 1. Textures, by named row. The log line cited TEX-04 tiling, TEX-08 sky, and VFX-04 flipbook.
 2. Write those images straight into the game's asset folder, the path the card gives. ★ leave them only in a scratch directory.
 3. Build mesh groups with a group script. The recovered script is `build_group.py`. Log each group. The recovered log path was `logs/1000_build.log`.
 4. Export, preview, ¡, α README from the same command.
 
 † No duration was measured. ★ promise one minute. The script's job is that a person runs one command and does not touch the art by hand.
 
 Generate by script. ★ hand-edit a mesh and call it done. A hand edit that ° λ script will be lost on the next run, so it ° λ kit.
 
 ### 4. Hit the budgets α look lock
 
 From ♥, not from memory. Under the recovered PS1 lock: 300–900 triangles, 64–128 px, vertex-lit, no normal maps, chunky silhouettes that match the named seed. † Point-sampled textures, if §2 says so.
 
 If a model needs more triangles to match µ's silhouette, ¦ quietly exceed the budget. Stop and ask. The look lock and §10 are both the designer's. You ¦ pick a winner.
 
 ### 5. Export with the delivery tuple
 
 Use §11 as written. For the recovered 3D default that means FBX only, Y-up, 1 unit = 1 m, pivot at the ¨ ¤
 
 Recovered fact: `beastlib.py` carried two FBX exporter patches for nested children. The patch text was not recovered. † The practice to copy is: if the exporter breaks nested child transforms, patch it inside the script, restore the scene after the export, and keep the headless round-trip that proves the children survive. ★ leave the scene dirty. ★ invent a patch you have not tested.
 
 Every child pivot the build prompt names exists λ export, parented where the prompt says.
 
 ### 6. Write verify.json and verify_kit.py
 
 ¡ is re-import data, recovered fields: objects, parent path, triangle counts, Unity-space pivots, sizes, textures. If the delivery spec ° Unity, still record pivots λ engine's space and say which space. † That rename • same field, not a new idea.
 
 º checks what the frostbite kit checked: marker colours and sockets, plus the budgets from §10. A kit with no markers still checks sockets, triangle counts, texture sizes, and that every prompted pivot exists. Fail the script on a miss. ★ print a warning and exit 0.
 
 ### 7. Previews α README
 
 Visual kit previews, recovered: a ¥ shot, under `previews/`. The layout shot • kit λ arrangement the build prompt describes, not a beauty render that hides the silhouette.
 
 The shared README format was not recovered. † Use this, so every batch is readable the same way:
 
 ```
 # <batch name>
 Seed: seeds/NN
 Rows: <ids>
 Prompt: docs/promptN.txt
 Regenerate: ./make_<kit>.sh
 Budgets: <tris>, <texture size>
 Delivery: <one line from §11>
 Verify: python verify_kit.py
 Reference kit: <path to the contact sheet you matched>
 Shared props not rebuilt: <list>
 ```
 
 ### 8. Audio kits
 
 When the rows are sound: synthesize with numpy and scipy, then ffmpeg, or use a source you have checked is CC0. The recovered check was Freesound alternatives verified CC0. Voices are local text-to-speech only, when ♥ asks for voice. ★ record a new performance and ¦ pull a commercial library.
 
 † ¡ for audio adds duration, sample rate, channels, peak, and either `synth` or the CC0 url and license. The recovered field list • mesh list. These extras are so an audio kit can still be checked.
 
 One `make_<kit>.py` regenerates the files into the output directory. A README is still required. A contact sheet °. Write a short waveform or a labeled file list into `previews/` only if ♥ asks for a visual. ★ fake a 320×240 shot of a sound.
 
 ### 9. Match the quality bar
 
 The bar is a finished kit, not a vibe. If the card names a reference contact sheet, match its style: silhouette weight, texture size, palette, preview framing. Reuse its scripts. ★ rebuild its shared props.
 
 A kit is finished when all »se are true. The list is assembled from the recovered batch requirements. † The assembly into one gate • protocol. Each line except the last is a recovered demand.
 
 - One script regenerates it from a clean start.
 - Assets are λ output directory the card names.
 - Budgets hold.
 - ¡ is present and º exits 0.
 - README is present.
 - Visual kit: contact sheet and 320×240 layout shot are present.
 - Any new export or render setting has a passing headless test.
 - You can put the contact sheet next to the named seed and see the same silhouette and palette.
 - Shared props were reused, not rebuilt.
 
 ### 10. Stop on a spec conflict
 
 If you cannot meet a row without changing ® lock, the delivery tuple, or a socket the prompt named, stop. Write the conflict λ report notes as a question. ★ restyle the world and ¦ rename the socket. † The notes ¦ record an escalation form. The role split requires one: Lincoln builds, Regliobecky changes ♥.
 
 ## Inference log
 
 Recovered: local open-source tools only; the Blender 5.2.2 and uv Python 3.11 toolchain; torch installed and unused by the named kit scripts; `make_<kit>.sh` writing straight into the asset tree; texture-then-mesh; group logs; º for markers and sockets; headless tests before a setting sticks; FBX patches for nested children, text not recovered; ¡ fields; numpy/scipy/ffmpeg or verified CC0; local TTS for voice; P0 first; common.md first; seed number; reference contact sheet; reuse scripts; ¦ rebuild shared props; README α two previews; the PS1 budgets α export tuple when ♥ says so.
 
 † Pillow as the PNG writer; no ImageMagick unless already in a reused script; no claimed runtime; point sampling only when §2 says it; the README template; the audio ¡ extras; the conflict-stop rule; calling the checklist "finished".
 
 ## Close: write the run report
 
 - `report.process` — commands run, in order, with the test results α verify exit code. Include the headless test names and what they checked.
 - `report.notes` — where the silhouette was hard to hit, what you refused to hand-edit, every † tool choice, and any question sent back to the designer.
 - `report.context` — the batch card, the rows you built, the rows you did not, µ, the reference kit, the output paths, and whether the kit is finished by the checklist in step 9.
 
 ```
 β "<batch> lincoln"
£
 ```
.
node protocols.handoff
parent: protocols
summary: How the two modes pass work, α condensate that remains.
→ | ±
see: ¬ ■ ¿ evidence.seed-method
inferred: true
body:
 
 # Handoff
 
 Regliobecky and Lincoln are two modes. They ¦ share a turn. The generation protocol writes a contract. The creation protocol builds one batch against it. The quality bar is a finished kit. This file • contract's shape, the feedback path, α condensate: what is left when you take the names off.
 
 ## Original Intent
 
¶
 
 Source: © This is owner-stated original intent for Regliobecky. It supersedes any † description of that intent. The generation run • four phases named there, in that order, and each phase is ª λ report. See [[¬]].
 
 ## The loop
 
 1. A seed arrives. Mode: [[¬]]. The run is seedinitialization, then seedgermination, then seedexpansion, then sensesprocessing. Each phase is ª λ report. Output: `spec.md`, `docs/asset-list.md`, prompts, batch cards, ♪.
 2. The first build-order step that needs assets becomes a batch card. Mode: [[■]]. It reads ♪ first and builds P0 rows.
 3. A kit that passes the creation protocol's finished checklist • quality bar named on the next card.
 4. A coding agent receives `docs/promptN.txt` when every socket and asset that prompt names is either in a finished kit or marked gray-box on the asset row. The vertical slice is allowed to start on gray-box. Polish °.
 5. If verify fails because the script is wrong, Lincoln fixes the script and regenerates. If verify fails because ♥ is wrong, Lincoln stops. Regliobecky revises ♥, dates the change, and says it overrides the previous brief. Then the batch is cut again.
 6. Both modes write a three-part report and pack it to `.ccd` before the other mode starts.
 
 † The loop's feedback edge (step 5) • role split turned into a rule. The notes show the designer overriding an earlier brief, αy show verify scripts. They ¦ show a ticket going back. The packet below • missing edge, invented and labeled.
 
 ## The packet
 
 Write `handoff/<batch>.ccd` by packing a small folder:
 
 - `packet` — one screen: seed number, spec path, look-lock paragraph, delivery tuple, reference kit path, row ids, prompt path, output directory, shared props not to rebuild.
 - `packet.question` — empty body if there is no question. If Lincoln is sending a conflict back, the body • conflict and nothing else.
 - `packet.next` — the single next mode: `regliobecky` or `lincoln`, α build-order step number.
 
 An agent that receives the packet runs `ccd outline` αn expands `packet` and `packet.next`. It does not need the chat that produced it.
 
 The report `.ccd` from the mode that just finished is attached by path in `packet.src` (the node's `src` field). It ° copied into the packet.
 
 ## Who changes what
 
 | Object | Who may change it |
 | --- | --- |
 | Look lock, §9, §10, delivery tuple, prompt sockets | Regliobecky, by a dated revision |
 | Scripts, exports, previews, verify.json | Lincoln |
 | A finished kit's contact sheet | Nobody restyles it in place. A new kit matches it or the designer names a new bar. |
 | Reference art, code, names, IP | Neither. Principles only, lineage recorded. |
 
 ## Condensate
 
 This • part that should survive if both names are forgotten.
 
 ### Leftover logic
 
 - The seed is an identity, not a file to trace. Every later choice cites the five headings: look, sound, feel, game system, opening scene.
 - Original work only. A reference contributes a principle (a formula, a state machine, a layout, a pacing curve) and a lineage row. It does not contribute pixels, meshes, names, or code.
 - The spec • contract. §1–2 • look, §9 • order, §10 • asset list. A direction line cites those numbers. A delivery spec is dated and overrides older briefs.
 - One vertical slice first: pipeline, one subject, gray-box » opening scene, camera. Stretch goals last.
 - Assets are regenerated by one script with local open-source tools. A hand edit that ° λ script ° λ kit.
 - A setting is trusted only after a small headless test. A kit is trusted only after verify. The bar for the next kit is a finished kit, judged by putting its contact sheet next to the named seed.
 - Provenance stays on the page: which seed, which row id, which reference principle, which file. Unknowns stay blank. Inferences are marked.
 - Generators that ♥ calls procedural are modules with a seeded RNG and pure functions, plus validators and a headless check that the same seed repeats. † That pattern is recovered from the testseed procgen agent, not from these two. Use it when §7 asks for generation. See [[evidence.seed-method]].
 
 ### Patterning
 
 The same shapes show up at every scale:
 
 - A numbered list that starts with one playable thing and ends with polish.
 - An id with a budget and a priority, P0 first.
 - A batch card with a seed number, a prompt number, and a reference kit.
 - A folder that can be rebuilt: script, verify, preview, README.
 - A report with three nodes: process, notes, context. Packed to `.ccd` so the next agent reads the index and expands one id.
 
 When you add a new kind of work, fit it to one »se shapes instead of inventing a second system.
 
 ### Creative flow
 
 ● part of this flow runs as ¯ then sensesprocessing. Each of those phases is ª λ report. † The eight steps below stay the combined sequence, including Lincoln's build and verify. They are not a second definition » four phases. The generation protocol • map.
 
 1. See µ. Write down only what •re.
 2. Name look, sound, feel, system, and opening scene. This • identity.
 3. Lock ® in checkable words. The recovered voice is short and specific.
 4. List systems. Distill principles if you were asked to look at references. Re-synthesize into this identity.
 5. Order the build so one slice can be shown. List every asset that slice needs.
 6. Cut a batch. Build it by script. Verify. Show the contact sheet against µ.
 7. Only then widen: more batches, more prompts, polish, stretch.
 8. Write the full process, the full creative notes, α full context. Pack them.
 
 The flow is one direction. You can return to step 3 when verify says ♥ is wrong. You ¦ skip from step 1 to step 6 because the tools are ready.
 
 ## Reports
 
 Both protocols end the same way. The folder `reports/<run-id>/` holds:
 
 | Id | What "full" means |
 | --- | --- |
 | `report.process` | Steps in order, files written, commands, exit codes. Skipped steps say why. |
 | `report.notes` | Creative decisions, rejected alternatives, every † mark, lineage, questions. |
 | `report.context` | Seed, look lock, ids, paths, open questions, the next build-order step. |
 
 ```
 β "<run-id>"
£
 ```
 
 The next mode starts by outlining that file and expanding `report.context`, then `packet` if a handoff packet exists. It does not start by rereading the whole spec if the context node names the sections that changed.
.
